  "run_command": {
    "sha256": "83813501768501588f53fbfe847fcf863764a030b933505c71d6ada8c58cb683",
    "descriptions": [
      {
        "path": [
          "description"
        ],
        "key": "schema.tool.0054ff5a6a0abdb3",
        "present": true,
        "source": "Run one program with exact arguments and no shell: argv[0] is the program (PATH name or a path relative to cwd), every other item reaches it as one argument, byte for byte. Quotes, spaces, ;, $(), *, ~, backslashes and newlines are plain data, never expanded. Choose it for an ordinary program call (git show 'HEAD:a b.py', pytest -k x, ffmpeg ...); python for computation, loops or h; shell only when you want shell grammar (pipes, redirects, globs, &&, background &). It never falls back to shell. Routes: home (the Linux container, cwd defaults to /app, same rights and autocommit as shell) or, with task_id, a local Forge task (task root, admission, custody, Stop and its full log). Host and Windows tasks are refused before anything is sent: their backends take only shell strings; use coding_run there, or computer for the Windows laptop. At home timeout=0 uses the configured default (normally 30s), capped at 600s; in a task 0 means no deadline. stdin_text is sent exactly as UTF-8 then EOF; omitted means closed stdin. stdout and stderr arrive combined in one stream; the full output is kept. The reply ends with a [run_command {...}] facts line (executor, cwd, argv, resolved program, exit_code or signal, timed_out and tree_stop), [changes] and [exit N]. [exit unobserved] means no exit status was seen (timeout): never read it as success. refused: nothing was launched (bad argv, missing program or cwd). in_doubt: it may have run; check before repeating. A non-zero exit does not mean nothing changed. Optional env, outputs and scratch: see their fields; the facts line reports them."
